      "id": "part_02-ch_05",
      "position": 7,
      "kind": "chapter",
      "prefix": "Chapter 5",
      "title": "The River of Life",
      "summary": "William Harvey rejected ancient Galenic doctrines about the creation and consumption of blood. Mathematical calculations proved the heart pumps several hundred pints of blood every single hour. Harvey demonstrated that blood circulates continuously in a closed loop through arteries and veins.",
      "content": "For fifteen hundred years, European doctors followed the ancient physician Galen. Galen taught that the human liver made blood from digested food. People believed that our organs burned blood like wood in fire.\n\nAccording to this old idea, blood flowed outward and simply vanished. The heart was not considered a pump for moving liquid. Instead, people thought the heart was an oven for warm spirits.\n\nIn England, a curious doctor named William Harvey doubted this old story. Harvey studied medicine at the famous universities of Cambridge and Padua. He spent years watching the beating hearts of living animals.\n\nHarvey noticed that the heart was a strong, muscular organ. It contracted and relaxed with steady, rhythmic power like a pump. Inside the heart, tiny flap valves opened in only one direction.\n\nThese small valves stopped blood from ever flowing backward inside the body. Next, Harvey decided to test Galen’s theory with simple mathematics. The results of his calculations surprised him and proved Galen wrong.\n\nAn adult heart pumps blood with seventy beats every single minute. That meant the heart pushed hundreds of pints of blood each hour. A human liver could never make so much liquid from daily meals.\n\nThe human body could also not absorb that giant river of blood. Harvey realized there was only one possible answer to this puzzle. The blood must move through the body in a closed, endless circle.\n\nBlood left the heart through thick arteries and traveled to the hands. Then, thin veins carried the same blood back to the heart. The pump pushed the liquid again and again every minute.\n\nHarvey proved his idea with a famous experiment on a man’s arm. He tied a tight cloth band around the man’s upper arm. Soon, the veins in the lower arm swelled with trapped blood.\n\nHarvey pressed his finger on a vein and pushed the blood down. The vein remained completely flat until he lifted his finger away. This test proved that blood in veins flows only toward the heart.\n\nIn 1628, Harvey published his discoveries in a famous medical book. Many traditional doctors were angry and shouted against his new ideas. But Harvey’s clear experiments and exact numbers could not be defeated.\n\nWilliam Harvey changed medicine from old opinions into an exact physical science. Today, every modern doctor relies on his knowledge of the human heart. His discovery showed that our blood is a moving river of life."
