      "id": "part_03-ch_09",
      "position": 12,
      "kind": "chapter",
      "prefix": "Chapter 9",
      "title": "Continents on the Move",
      "summary": "Alfred Wegener observed matching coastlines and identical fossils across distant oceans. He proposed that all landmasses once formed a single ancient supercontinent called Pangaea. Mid-ocean ridge discoveries and seabed spreading confirmed continental drift as plate tectonics.",
      "content": "Looking at a world map, schoolchildren often see an interesting puzzle. The eastern edge of South America fits cleanly into western Africa. The two giant continents look like matching pieces of a jigsaw puzzle.\n\nFor hundreds of years, older geologists ignored this matching shape. They believed that continents were heavy rocks that never moved at all. They thought the continents stood frozen in place forever.\n\nIn 1910, a German weather scientist decided to study the map. His name was Alfred Wegener, and he loved polar exploration. Wegener believed that the matching coastlines were not an accident.\n\nWegener searched through library books for clues about ancient animals and plants. He discovered identical fossils of a small swimming reptile in South America and Africa. The creature could never swim across thousands of miles of ocean.\n\nHe also found ancient tropical plant fossils in the cold ice of Antarctica. Furthermore, the rocky mountains in North America matched the rocks in Scotland. The mountains had the exact same layers of stone and coal.\n\nIn 1912, Wegener announced his famous idea of continental drift. He proposed that all continents once formed a single giant supercontinent. He gave this ancient supercontinent the name *Pangaea*, meaning all lands.\n\nOver millions of years, Pangaea slowly broke apart into pieces. The continents drifted across the globe like giant icebergs on water. However, other scientists laughed at Wegener and mocked his theory.\n\nBecause Wegener could not explain how continents moved, few people believed him. Sadly, Wegener died during a cold blizzard in Greenland in 1930. He never saw his radical theory accepted by the scientific world.\n\nYears later, in the 1950s, new technology explored the dark ocean floor. A geologist named Marie Tharp used sound waves to map the Atlantic seabed. She discovered a giant mountain chain with a deep valley in the center.\n\nHot liquid rock constantly rose from inside Earth into this underwater valley. The cooling rock created new ocean floor and pushed the continents apart. Deep ocean trenches pulled old ocean floor back down into hot rock.\n\nBy the late 1960s, scientists fully accepted the theory of plate tectonics. Wegener’s continental drift was proven right by modern instruments and underwater maps. The plates of Earth move slowly, as fast as human fingernails grow.\n\nToday, plate tectonics explains how earthquakes happen and how tall mountains rise. Earth is not a cold, frozen rock in space. Our dynamic planet is constantly moving and changing under our feet."
